Optionally correct the antenna period error of the centroid (TipSignalReference, CellCorrection*, CentroidFadeMin/Max)

The position between two antennas is interpolated from three amplitudes, and neither the parabola
nor the centroid is linear in the true position: a slow straight diagonal stroke comes out wavy,
once per antenna. Measured on the Surface Pro 9 with the Slim Pen 2 on 20 strokes of three captures
drawn along a straight edge, at 13 to 76 mm/s (deviation from the fitted line, split into the part
that repeats with the X and with the Y antennas, amplitude in mm on that axis):

- parabola on X, centroid on Y: X 0.07 .. 0.10 at up to 26 degrees of tilt, 0.26 .. 0.48 at 40 to
  48 degrees (plus 0.08 .. 0.32 twice per antenna, where a neighbour antenna in antiphase pins the
  parabola to the cell border for a third of the cell); Y 0.06 .. 0.28.
- centroid on both axes: X 0.04 .. 0.28, Y 0.05 .. 0.25, almost only the first harmonic.

Nothing else contributes: the tilt offset and its smoothing add 0.002 .. 0.013 mm, the jitter lock
0 .. 0.014 mm, and the two MPP v2 position windows agree.

The error of the centroid changes with two things that can be read from the same window:

  level     = summed amplitude of the three antennas, mean of both axes / TipSignalReference
              (grows with pressure and tilt)
  asymmetry = (sum of the three antennas beyond the upper neighbour - sum of the three beyond the
              lower one, with their sign relative to the strongest antenna) / summed amplitude of
              the three antennas in the middle, smoothed over about ten samples
              (the tail of the tip signal falls to zero and below on the side the pen leans to)

  error = (Cos + CosLevel (level - 1) + CosAsymmetry asymmetry) cos(2 pi x)
        + (Sin + SinLevel (level - 1) + SinAsymmetry asymmetry) sin(2 pi x)
        + Cos2 cos(4 pi x) + Sin2 sin(4 pi x)                       (x and error in antennas)

is subtracted from the centroid (per axis: CellCorrectionCosX .. CellCorrectionSin2Y; the level
is limited to CellCorrectionLevelMin .. Max, the asymmetry to CellCorrectionAsymmetryMax). Neither
the level nor the asymmetry alone is enough: a pen at 45 to 50 degrees can have the level of a pen
at 20 degrees (1.2) with the error of a firmly pressed one (0.24 mm), which only the asymmetry
shows; pressure at constant tilt changes the error with the level.

With the coefficients fitted to all strokes (these 20 and the two fast ones below), the 20 give X 0.001 .. 0.063 mm and
Y 0.007 .. 0.059 mm, and the deviation from the line falls from 0.07 .. 0.35 mm rms to 0.03 ..
0.10 mm, most of which is the hand. With the coefficients fitted to the strokes of the other
captures only: 0.003 .. 0.090 mm per axis, except the one stroke at 45 to 50 degrees with a low
level, whose combination of level and asymmetry occurs in one capture only (0.14 .. 0.17 mm
then, 0.24 .. 0.25 mm uncorrected).

Two strokes at 200 mm/s with light pressure do not follow: there the centroid has 0.02 .. 0.03 mm
on X without the correction and 0.09 .. 0.12 mm with it (parabola 0.12 .. 0.16 mm), and 0.06 ..
0.12 mm on Y either way. Light upright writing and a pen leaning to another side than the lower
right are not covered by a measurement.

The correction applies where the centroid is selected (PositionCentroidX / Y) and adds no delay.
Everything else of a sample is unchanged.

CentroidFadeMin / CentroidFadeMax: with a weak signal (pen hovering high) the centroid jumps, one
position in ten is off by 0.4 .. 1.3 mm from its neighbours where the parabola is off by 0.14 ..
0.28 mm. Below CentroidFadeMin (in units of TipSignalReference) the parabola is used, above
CentroidFadeMax the centroid, in between a mix.

All off by default.

